  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Natural-History Plate",
    "overview": "**Creative North Star: \"The Natural-History Plate\"**\n\nSub-Sub is drawn as an engraved plate from an 1839 natural history: a cool grey-green sheet, a lighter plate field inside a plate mark, copperplate ink for type and fine rules, and a few transparent hand-coloured washes as the only colour. The product is shown as a specimen. Its parts carry figure numbers in the margin and leader lines, and an italic caption below explains them. Every claim is a figure that can be checked.\n\nThe same world runs on four surfaces in four registers: the website (persuade, then read), the tutorial (read), the workshop slides (present) and the browser view (operate). Dark mode is the steel plate itself, with a blue-black ground, pale engraved line and brighter washes. The density is that of a printed paper. Type does the work and colour marks meaning. Decoration is limited to what an engraver would cut.\n\nThe world refuses the category's split hero (headline beside a screenshot, feature cards below) and the usual AI design tropes. It must also not become precious: the controls stay plain and legible.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Washes are the only colour, and each wash has one meaning.",
      "State is drawn by line: solid, dashed, dotted, struck.",
      "Running heads in small capitals replace labels above headings.",
      "A serif with a true italic for text and captions, a hyperlegible sans for controls.",
      "Square corners, flat surfaces, fine rules."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Wash, One Meaning Rule",
        "body": "Prussian blue is the Researcher and links; madder is the Librarian; sap green is applied, checked or full text; gamboge is preview, pending or abstract. No wash is used for decoration or for a second meaning.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Ink Error Rule",
        "body": "An error is drawn in ink: a hollow mark and a word, in the text colour, at weight 600. Errors are never red, because madder belongs to the Librarian.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Struck Removal Rule",
        "body": "A removal is struck through (1px line-through) in muted ink, with no fill. It is never coloured.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Own Colour Rule",
        "body": "Each mode button in the browser view keeps its own colour whether it is chosen or not. The chosen one also gets its wash and a 2px underline in its colour.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Roman Heading Rule",
        "body": "Headings are roman. True italics are for captions, figure numbers and labels, quotes and blockquotes, the subtitle, and the middle of a running head.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Small Capitals Rule",
        "body": "Plate numbers, figure references, table heads and running heads use real small capitals (all-small-caps, 0.07 to 0.08em tracking), never uppercase letters with tracking.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Unbroken Name Rule",
        "body": "The name Sub-Sub never breaks, least of all at its hyphen (white-space: nowrap on every occurrence in running text).",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Engraved Surface Rule",
        "body": "No drop shadows, no blur, no glassmorphism, no gradients. The one exception is the fine engraved hatch (1px lines every 3px at 9% ink) on the drawn window bar of Plate I.",
        "section": "elevation"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Line State Rule",
        "body": "State is drawn by line before colour: solid for full text and applied, dashed for abstract and preview, dotted for metadata, struck through for removed or done. The same four lines appear on the website's Plate II, in the browser view's evidence line, on the workshop's evidence ladder and in the tutorial's checklist.",
        "section": "shapes"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** give each wash one meaning, and use the same meaning on every surface.",
      "**Do** draw state by line (solid, dashed, dotted, struck) so it reads without colour.",
      "**Do** open a plate or page with a running head in small capitals over a 1px ink rule.",
      "**Do** keep headings roman and keep true italics for captions, figure labels, quotes and running-head middles.",
      "**Do** set tables as in a printed paper: heavy rules above and below, a fine rule under the head, small-caps head.",
      "**Do** keep the name Sub-Sub on one line.",
      "**Do** offer reading mode for long text: 1.2rem, 1.9 leading, 0.06em word spacing, 36rem measure, remembered across visits.",
      "**Do** give every figure a text equivalent and keep every interaction usable by keyboard and touch."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** put kickers, eyebrows or any label above a heading; the running head does that job.",
      "**Don't** use red, or madder, for errors or removals; errors are ink with a hollow mark and a word, removals are struck.",
      "**Don't** use shadows, blur, glass or gradients; the only gradient is the engraved hatch on Plate I's window bar.",
      "**Don't** round corners past 3px or use pill shapes for buttons and fields.",
      "**Don't** use uppercase with tracking for labels; use small capitals.",
      "**Don't** set the h1 in italic.",
      "**Don't** use a split hero with feature cards, or emoji, anywhere."
    ]
  }
}